---
// REVIEW: Layer 2 interpretation for the Orthostatic Grading Scale (OGS),
// drafted for Craig's review. Static, rendered at build time. FAQ answers come
// from orthostatic-grading-scale.content.ts and are shown word for word (they
// also feed the page's FAQPage JSON-LD).
import { FAQ, REFERENCE, SOURCES } from '../../tools/orthostatic-grading-scale.content';
import RelatedTools from '../RelatedTools.astro';
import '../../styles/interpretation.css';

const s = SOURCES;
---

<!-- REVIEW: new copy for Craig's review. -->
<section class="interpretation" aria-labelledby="understanding-ogs">
	<h2 id="understanding-ogs">Understanding your OGS score</h2>

	<h3 id="what-it-measures">What the score measures</h3>
	<p>{FAQ.measures.a}</p>
	<p>
		In the original study of 145 people tested in the Mayo autonomic laboratory, the five questions hung together well
		(alpha 0.91), and scores tracked laboratory measures of autonomic function, most closely the adrenergic (blood
		pressure control) part (<a href={s.schrezenmaier.href} rel="noopener">{s.schrezenmaier.label}</a>).
	</p>
	<p>{FAQ.pots.a} (<a href={s.knoop.href} rel="noopener">{s.knoop.label}</a>)</p>

	<h3 id="score-bands">Orthostatic Grading Scale score interpretation</h3>
	<p>{FAQ.high.a}</p>
	<p>
		The thresholds of 4 and 9 are as stated by <a href={s.slomko.href} rel="noopener">{s.slomko.label}</a>; we could not
		check them against the full text of the original paper. Mild, moderate and severe labels sometimes quoted for the OGS
		have no published thresholds we could verify, so they are not used here.
	</p>
	<table class="interp-table">
		<caption>Average OGS totals in published studies</caption>
		<thead>
			<tr><th scope="col">Group</th><th scope="col">People</th><th scope="col">Average</th><th scope="col">Source</th></tr>
		</thead>
		<tbody>
			{REFERENCE.map(r => (
				<tr><th scope="row">{r.group}</th><td>{r.n}</td><td>{r.mean}</td><td><a href={s[r.source].href} rel="noopener">{s[r.source].label}</a></td></tr>
			))}
		</tbody>
	</table>

	<h3 id="change">What a change in score means</h3>
	<p>{FAQ.change.a}</p>
	<p>
		Your saved results above keep the history on this device; keep your
		<a href="/tools/trackers/stand-test-logger/">stand test</a> readings alongside.
	</p>

	<h3 id="what-it-does-not-measure">What it does not measure</h3>
	<p>{FAQ.diagnosis.a}</p>
	<p>
		The <a href="/tools/nasa-lean-test/">NASA lean test</a> records those readings at home, and the
		<a href="/evidence/30-bpm-threshold-pots/">30 bpm threshold explainer</a> covers what they mean. The
		<a href="/tools/malmo-pots-score/">Malmö POTS score</a> covers a wider set of symptoms over the past week.
	</p>

	<h3 id="taking-it-to-your-gp">Taking this to your GP or consultant</h3>
	<p>
		Bring your total, the date, your answer to the standing-time question and, if you have it, the trend from your saved
		results. Describe the OGS as a five-question Mayo Clinic scale for symptoms on standing, used in POTS and ME/CFS
		research. A score of 9 or more, with symptoms on standing, supports asking for a lying and standing blood pressure and
		heart rate check. It does not show what is causing the symptoms. See
		<a href="/testing/preparing-for-gp-appointment/">preparing for a GP appointment</a>.
	</p>

	<RelatedTools path="/tools/orthostatic-grading-scale/" />

	<div class="tool-foot">
		<slot name="disclaimer" />
		<details class="sources" id="sources">
			<summary>Sources and licence</summary>
			<slot name="citation" />
			<ul class="interp-sources">
				<li>Schrezenmaier C, Gehrking JA, Hines SM, Low PA, Benrud-Larson LM, Sandroni P. Evaluation of orthostatic hypotension: relationship of a new self-report instrument to laboratory-based measures. <em>Mayo Clin Proc</em> 2005;80(3):330–334. <a href={s.schrezenmaier.href} rel="noopener">Article</a></li>
				<li>Słomko J, et al. Autonomic phenotypes in chronic fatigue syndrome (CFS) are associated with illness severity: a cluster analysis. <em>J Clin Med</em> 2020;9(8):2531. <a href={s.slomko.href} rel="noopener">Full text</a></li>
				<li>Knoop I, et al. Validation of symptom measures in patients under investigation for postural orthostatic tachycardia syndrome (POTS): the Orthostatic Grading Scale (OGS) and the Symptom Screen for Small-fiber Polyneuropathy (SSS). <em>Auton Neurosci</em> 2023. <a href={s.knoop.href} rel="noopener">Article</a></li>
				<li>Issa A, et al. Autonomic dysfunction in myalgic encephalomyelitis/chronic fatigue syndrome (ME/CFS): findings from the Multi-Site Clinical Assessment of ME/CFS (MCAM) study in the USA. <em>J Clin Med</em> 2025;14(17):6269. <a href={s.issa.href} rel="noopener">Full text</a></li>
				<li>Peebles KC, et al. The prevalence and impact of orthostatic intolerance in young women across the hypermobility spectrum. <em>Am J Med Genet A</em> 2022;188(6):1761. <a href={s.peebles.href} rel="noopener">Full text</a></li>
				<li>McDonald C, Koshi S, Busner L, Kavi L, Newton JL. Postural tachycardia syndrome is associated with significant symptoms and functional impairment predominantly affecting young women: a UK perspective. <em>BMJ Open</em> 2014;4:e004127. <a href={s.mcdonald.href} rel="noopener">Full text</a></li>
				<li>Domingo JC, et al. Association of circulating biomarkers with illness severity measures differentiates myalgic encephalomyelitis/chronic fatigue syndrome and post-COVID-19 condition: a prospective pilot cohort study. <em>J Transl Med</em> 2024;22:343. <a href={s.domingo.href} rel="noopener">Full text</a></li>
			</ul>
		</details>
	</div>
</section>
